routes.brief:
This route adds sources to the Part II of Hochschild, cyclotomic and refined trace methods proposed by the Clausen–Mathew–Morrow extraction (RefinedTraceMethodsPartIIHenselianPairs). That Part II already owns rigidity of K^inv for henselian pairs (Theorem A), the finiteness of TC/p (Theorem G), Theorem C, the Geisser–Levine and Geisser–Hesselholt theorems, the mod p cohomological dimension of F_p-algebras and rigidity along extensions of separably closed fields. Clausen–Mathew use exactly these to identify the stalks of Selmer K-theory (Theorem 6.11), to prove Lichtenbaum–Quillen for fields of characteristic p (Theorem 6.13) and to reduce Theorem 6.18 to fields.

Export, in these forms:
- rigidity for connective E_1-rings whose π_0 is commutative and henselian along an ideal (via Dundas–Goodwillie–McCarthy);
- TC/p commuting with filtered colimits of connective E_1-rings;
- K_{≥0}(A)_p̂ ≃ TC(A)_p̂ for strictly henselian A of residue characteristic p;
- Geisser–Levine and Geisser–Hesselholt for fields k of characteristic p, with the truncation bound log_p[k : k^p] and the trace as the inclusion of Ω^i_log;
- H^i(Spec(R)_et, F) = 0 for i > 1, R an F_p-algebra and F p-torsion;
- Gabber–Suslin invariance of K_{≥0}/p along maps of strictly henselian local Z[1/p]-algebras (Gabber rigidity from KTheoryFiniteLocalFields L.2 plus Proposition 6.2 of Clausen–Mathew–Morrow).

In return, that Part II's item 111 imports Theorem 5.16 (TC/p is an étale Postnikov sheaf) from the Selmer K-theory Part II and the Nisnevich homotopy-dimension theorem from the EnhancedDerivedSheaves Part II, both proposed by this extraction. Unit tests: the strict henselization of Z_p (Theorem C), F_p(t) (Geisser–Levine), and the extension F̄_p ⊂ F̄_p(t)^sep of separably closed fields.
